            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Peace (rooted in Hebrew 'shalom') primarily signifies comprehensive wholeness, well-being, and reconciled relationship with God, rather than merely the absence of conflict. It is also commonly understood to encompass inner emotional tranquility, social harmony, and eschatological restoration.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Peace is covenant wholeness restored through righteousness, entailing completion, restoration, and repayment, and is explicitly distinguished from a mere truce or feeling.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Comprehensive restored wholeness and reconciled relationships.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Covenant wholeness restored through righteousness (completion, restoration, repayment).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book explicitly excludes 'feeling' from the definition of biblical peace, whereas the consensus recognizes inner tranquility, comfort, and emotional calm as a widespread and valid complementary facet of the term.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. Both agree that peace is primarily restored wholeness (shalom) rather than the mere cessation of external conflict (a truce), but the book applies stricter conditions regarding covenant righteousness and repayment.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book builds on the consensus understanding of peace as 'wholeness' rather than 'absence of conflict', but it refines the concept by anchoring it strictly to covenant conditions and righteousness, explicitly ruling out subjective feelings.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The New Testament frequently uses 'peace' (eirēnē) to describe a subjective state of comfort, lack of anxiety, or inner guard (e.g., Philippians 4:7, John 14:27), which challenges the strict exclusion of 'feeling' from the symbol's referents.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
